@subsection Example: Arithmetic Operations for Rational Numbers

Suppose we want to do arithmetic with rational numbers.  We want to be able to
add, subtract, multiply, and divide them and to test whether two rational
numbers are equal.

Let us begin by assuming that we already have a way of constructing a rational
number from a numerator and a denominator.  We also assume that, given a
rational number, we have a way of extracting (or selecting) its numerator and
its denominator.  Let us further assume that the constructor and selectors are
available as procedures:

@itemize @bullet

@item
@code{Rational::new(⟨@var{n}⟩, ⟨@var{d}⟩)} returns the rational number whose
numerator is the integer @code{⟨@var{n}⟩} and whose denominator is the integer
@code{⟨@var{d}⟩}.

@item
@code{⟨@var{x}⟩.numer()} returns the numerator of the rational number
@code{⟨@var{x}⟩}.

@item
@code{⟨@var{x}⟩.denom()} returns the denominator of the rational number
@code{⟨@var{x}⟩}.

@end itemize

@noindent
We are using here a powerful strategy of synthesis: @newterm{wishful thinking}.
We haven't yet said how a rational number is represented, or how the methods
@code{numer}, @code{denom}, and @code{new} should be implemented.  Even
so, if we did have these three, we could then add, subtract,
multiply, divide, and test equality by using the following relations:
@displaymath
\eqalign{
{n_1 \over d_1} + {n_2 \over d_2}       &= {n_1 d_2 + n_2 d_1 \over d_1 d_2}, \cr
{n_1 \over d_1} - {n_2 \over d_2} 	&= {n_1 d_2 - n_2 d_1 \over d_1 d_2}, \cr
{n_1 \over d_1} \times {n_2 \over d_2} 	&= {n_1 n_2 \over d_1 d_2}, \cr
{n_1 \,/\, d_1} \over {n_2 \,/\, d_2} 	&= {n_1 d_2 \over d_1 n_2}, \cr
{n_1 \over d_1} 			&= {n_2 \over d_2} \quad
						{\rm\ if\ and\ only\ if\quad} 
						n_1 d_2 = n_2 d_1. 
}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
n_1   n_2   n_1 d_2 + n_2 d_1
--- + --- = -----------------
d_1   d_2        d_1 d_2

n_1   n_2   n_1 d_2 - n_2 d_1
--- - --- = -----------------
d_1   d_2        d_1 d_2

n_1   n_2   n_1 n_2
--- * --- = -------
d_1   d_2   d_1 d_2

n_1 / d_1   n_1 d_2
--------- = -------
n_2 / d_2   d_1 n_2

n_1   n_2
--- = ---  if and only if n_1 d_2 = n_2 d_1
d_1   d_2
@end example

@end ifinfo
@noindent
We can express these rules as methods on @code{Rational}, checked so that an
overflowing numerator or denominator is reported rather than silently wrapped:

@example rust
impl Rational @{
    pub fn add(&self, other: &Self) -> Result<Self, SchemeError> @{
        let num = checked(self.num * other.den + other.num * self.den);
        let den = checked(self.den * other.den);
        Self::new(num?, den?)
    @}

    pub fn sub(&self, other: &Self) -> Result<Self, SchemeError> @{
        let num = checked(self.num * other.den - other.num * self.den);
        let den = checked(self.den * other.den);
        Self::new(num?, den?)
    @}

    pub fn mul(&self, other: &Self) -> Result<Self, SchemeError> @{
        Self::new(checked(self.num * other.num)?, checked(self.den * other.den)?)
    @}

    pub fn div(&self, other: &Self) -> Result<Self, SchemeError> @{
        Self::new(checked(self.num * other.den)?, checked(self.den * other.num)?)
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
(@code{checked} stands for this edition's checked-arithmetic policy: exact
integers are @code{i128}, and every multiplication or addition that could
overflow returns a @code{Result} instead of wrapping.  The real definitions,
using @code{i128::checked_mul} and @code{checked_add} directly, are in
@code{ch02::sec_2_1}.)  Equality, @code{equal-rat?}, does not need a method of
its own: once every @code{Rational} is built through the reduced, canonical
constructor below, Rust's derived @code{PartialEq} on the two private fields
@emph{is} @code{n_1 d_2 = n_2 d_1}.

@noindent
Now we have the operations on rational numbers defined in terms of the selector
and constructor methods @code{numer}, @code{denom}, and @code{new}.
But we haven't yet defined these.  What we need is some way to glue together a
numerator and a denominator to form a rational number.

@subsubheading Pairs

To enable us to implement the concrete level of our data abstraction, this
edition provides a compound structure called a @newterm{pair}, which can be
constructed with the function @code{cons}.  This function takes two
arguments and returns a compound data object that contains the two arguments as
parts.  Given a pair, we can extract the parts using the functions
@code{car} and @code{cdr}.@footnote{The name @code{cons} stands for
``construct.''  The names @code{car} and @code{cdr} derive from the original
implementation of Lisp on the IBM 704.  That machine had an addressing scheme
that allowed one to reference the ``address'' and ``decrement'' parts of a
memory location.  @code{Car} stands for ``Contents of Address part of
Register'' and @code{cdr} (pronounced ``could-er'') stands for ``Contents of
Decrement part of Register.''} Thus, we can use @code{cons}, @code{car}, and
@code{cdr} as follows:

@example rust
let x = cons(1, 2);

car(&x)
// => 1

cdr(&x)
// => 2
@end example

@noindent
Notice that a pair is a data object that can be given a name and manipulated,
just like a primitive data object.  Moreover, @code{cons} can be used to form
pairs whose elements are pairs, and so on:

@example rust
let x = cons(1, 2);
let y = cons(3, 4);
let z = cons(x, y);

car(&car(&z))
// => 1

car(&cdr(&z))
// => 3
@end example

@noindent
In @ref{2.2} we will see how this ability to combine pairs means that
pairs can be used as general-purpose building blocks to create all sorts of
complex data structures.  The single compound-data primitive @newterm{pair},
implemented by @code{cons}, @code{car}, and @code{cdr}, is the
only glue we need.  Data objects constructed from pairs are called
@newterm{list-structured} data.

@subsubheading Representing rational numbers

Pairs offer a natural way to complete the rational-number system.  Simply
represent a rational number as a pair of two integers: a numerator and a
denominator.  Then @code{new}, @code{numer}, and @code{denom} are readily
implemented as follows:@footnote{This edition does not shorten
@code{new}/@code{numer}/@code{denom} to bare aliases of @code{cons}/@code{car}/
@code{cdr} the way the original text's footnote considers, because in Rust
@code{Rational} is its own struct, not a pair with a borrowed name; the
distinction the footnote draws (one function called versus two, and whether a
debugger can watch the constructor alone) is a property of the original
Scheme technique and does not carry over.}

@example rust
struct Rational @{ num: i128, den: i128 @}

impl Rational @{
    fn new(n: i128, d: i128) -> Self @{
        Rational @{ num: n, den: d @}
    @}

    fn numer(&self) -> i128 @{ self.num @}
    fn denom(&self) -> i128 @{ self.den @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
Also, in order to display the results of our computations, we print
rational numbers by printing the numerator, a slash, and the
denominator, through Rust's @code{Display} trait:@footnote{@code{Display} is
this edition's mechanism for printing data, the counterpart of the Scheme
primitive @code{display}.  Implementing @code{Display} for @code{Rational}
lets @code{println!("@{@}", x)} and @code{x.to_string()} both reach the same
formatting code, so there is only the one definition below, not a
@code{print-rat} that calls a lower-level @code{display} primitive.}

@example rust
impl fmt::Display for Rational @{
    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> fmt::Result @{
        write!(f, "@{@}/@{@}", self.num, self.den)
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
Now we can try our rational-number procedures:

@example rust
let one_half = Rational::new(1, 2);
println!("@{one_half@}");
// => 1/2

let one_third = Rational::new(1, 3);
println!("@{@}", one_half.add(&one_third)?);
// => 5/6

println!("@{@}", one_half.mul(&one_third)?);
// => 1/6

println!("@{@}", one_third.add(&one_third)?);
// => 6/9
@end example

@noindent
As the final example shows, our rational-number implementation does not reduce
rational numbers to lowest terms.  We can remedy this by changing
@code{new}. If we have a @code{gcd} function like the one in
@ref{1.2.5} that produces the greatest common divisor of two integers, we can
use @code{gcd} to reduce the numerator and the denominator to lowest terms
before constructing the value:

@example rust
impl Rational @{
    fn new(n: i128, d: i128) -> Self @{
        let g = gcd(n.abs(), d.abs());
        Rational @{ num: n / g, den: d / g @}
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
Now we have

@example rust
println!("@{@}", one_third.add(&one_third)?);
// => 2/3
@end example

@noindent
as desired.  This modification was accomplished by changing the constructor
@code{new} without changing any of the methods (such as @code{add} and
@code{mul}) that implement the actual operations.  (This edition's checked,
overflow-reporting constructor lives at @code{ch02::sec_2_1::Rational::new};
the fragment above simplifies it, dropping the @code{Result} return type, to
keep the change to @code{new} visible on its own.  The runnable listing of
this subsection, @code{examples/sec_2_1_1_rational.rs}, uses the real, checked
constructor throughout, including for the unreduced 6/9 case above, which it
recomputes directly from the addition formula rather than through a second,
unreduced constructor.)

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.1}Exercise 2.1:} Define a better version of
@code{Rational::new} that handles both positive and negative arguments.  The
constructor should normalize the sign so that if the rational number is
positive, both the numerator and denominator are positive, and if the rational
number is negative, only the numerator is negative.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.1a}Exercise 2.1a:} @emph{Exercise 2.1a is added by
this edition and extends exercise 2.1; SICP numbers stop at 2.1.}  This
edition's numbers convention makes every rational-number operation checked:
@code{Rational::add}, @code{sub}, @code{mul}, and @code{div} all return
@code{Result<Rational, SchemeError>}, reporting @code{SchemeError::Overflow}
rather than wrapping when a numerator or denominator would not fit an
@code{i128}.  Repeatedly square the rational number @code{99/100} (that is,
compute @code{r}, then @code{r * r}, then @code{(r * r) * (r * r)}, and so on)
and find the first squaring that overflows.  Since 99 and 100 share no common
factor, the @code{gcd} step of @code{new} never actually reduces anything in
this particular chain, so the answer follows from the size of @code{99^(2^k)}
alone.
@end quotation

